The code statements that lead to the bug are:
```c
if (rm != ARM_R0)
    emit(ARM_MOV_R(ARM_R0, rm), ctx);
```
```c
if (rn != ARM_R1)
    emit(ARM_MOV_R(ARM_R1, rn), ctx);
```
Reason:
The bug occurs because `emit_udiv()` was called with `rn` set to `ARM_R0` (the scratch register) and `rm` set to another register. When `rm` is loaded into `ARM_R0` before `rn`, it overwrites the value in `ARM_R0`, causing both the dividend and divisor to be the same when `jit_udiv()` is called, leading to incorrect division results.

The code statements that fix the bug are:
```c
if (rn != ARM_R1)
    emit(ARM_MOV_R(ARM_R1, rn), ctx);
```
```c
if (rm != ARM_R0)
    emit(ARM_MOV_R(ARM_R0, rm), ctx);
```
Reason:
The fix involves loading `rn` into `ARM_R1` first, ensuring that `ARM_R0` retains its value as the dividend. This prevents the accidental overwriting of `ARM_R0` with `rm` before it is used as a source for the division operation, thus ensuring that the correct values are used for both the dividend and divisor in the `jit_udiv()` function.

